# Read only by this platform's build files -- platform_config.cmake, which
# injects the redirect, and CMakeLists.txt, which links the pool. Nothing in
# src/ references it, so it does not belong under ENABLE_AI_PLAYER's Decoder
# Options menu in src/audio_player/Kconfig; no other platform selects it.
config MP3_DECODER_STATIC_BUF
    bool "MP3_DECODER_STATIC_BUF: allocate minimp3 scratch/context from a dedicated internal-RAM pool"
    default n
    help
      Allocate the MP3 decoder's per-frame scratch and its long-lived context
      from a small dedicated internal-RAM pool (mcu/src/mp3_internal_pool.c,
      ~23KB) instead of the general purpose heap.

      src/audio_player is untouched upstream and knows nothing about this:
      decoder_mp3.c and minimp3.h both name tal_psram_malloc/tal_psram_free
      directly under ENABLE_EXT_RAM. The platform redirects them by force-
      including a header that redefines those two names (see
      platform_config.cmake), scoped to decoder_mp3.c alone so that every other
      tal_psram_malloc caller still gets PSRAM.

      This is about memory latency, not heap pressure. Left alone the decoder
      allocates from PSRAM, and decoding out of PSRAM cannot keep up:
      mp3dec_scratch_t (grbuf/syn, ~16KB) is read and written throughout every
      frame, which stutters audibly on SiWx917. The pool sits in internal RAM,
      removing the PSRAM access latency.

      It is a dedicated pool rather than the shared FreeRTOS heap
      (pvPortMalloc/ucHeap) on purpose: that heap also serves task stacks and
      queues, and routing the decoder through it was tried and failed on real
      hardware -- a 16KB allocation returned NULL because nothing that size was
      free. Routing it to the newlib heap (_sbrk) was tried too and also
      rejected. This pool never competes with anything else.

      Costs ~23KB of internal RAM permanently (reserved for the pool, not
      freed back even when idle) and only ever holds one context + one
      scratch buffer at a time, so enable it only for single-stream playback.

